BT /F1 20 Tf 1 0 0 1 48 782 Tm (Speaker Notes) Tj ET
BT /F1 15 Tf 1 0 0 1 48 746 Tm (Slide 1 - Title) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 48 723 Tm (Start simple:) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 62 705 Tm (> This presentation is about the RL work I did for the Furuta inverted) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 62 691 Tm (> pendulum: what worked, what did not, what I learned, and how someone can) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 62 677 Tm (> continue from the GitHub repository.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 48 660 Tm (Do not overclaim. The honest headline is better:) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 62 642 Tm (> A simulation-trained TD3 policy transferred far enough to attempt swing-up on) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 62 628 Tm (> real hardware. The remaining problem is robust and quiet capture near upright.) Tj ET
BT /F1 15 Tf 1 0 0 1 48 604 Tm (Slide 2 - What Started The Project) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 48 581 Tm (Explain that the Furuta pendulum is a difficult nonlinear underactuated system:) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 62 564 Tm (- one motor at the rotary arm,) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 62 551 Tm (- pendulum is indirectly controlled,) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 62 538 Tm (- swing-up needs energy injection,) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 62 525 Tm (- balance needs fine stabilization.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 48 508 Tm (The goal was not only to get one lucky video. The goal became a workflow:) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 62 491 Tm (- train,) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 62 478 Tm (- evaluate,) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 62 465 Tm (- compare model and hardware,) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 62 452 Tm (- document,) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 62 439 Tm (- hand over.) Tj ET
BT /F1 15 Tf 1 0 0 1 48 415 Tm (Slide 3 - System And Control Path) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 48 392 Tm (Emphasize the action interpretation because this caused many questions:) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 62 374 Tm (> The RL agent outputs a normalized action. On the hardware-oriented path, that) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 62 360 Tm (> action becomes a current command. It is not a torque command directly on the) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 62 346 Tm (> real system.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 48 329 Tm (Mention:) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 62 312 Tm (- the uC has its own current PI loop,) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 62 299 Tm (- the RL policy does not need to run at the 20 kHz firmware rate,) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 62 286 Tm (- 200 Hz and 500 Hz policy rates were tested.) Tj ET
BT /F1 15 Tf 1 0 0 1 48 262 Tm (Slide 4 - Why TD3) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 48 239 Tm (Say TD3 is a practical continuous-action baseline:) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 62 222 Tm (- DDPG was too fragile in our early tests,) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 62 209 Tm (- TD3 keeps a deterministic actor but improves training stability with twin) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 48 196 Tm (  critics and delayed policy updates,) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 62 183 Tm (- actor-only deployment is convenient.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 48 166 Tm (Avoid going deep into math unless asked.) Tj ET
BT /F1 15 Tf 1 0 0 1 48 142 Tm (Slide 4b - DDPG vs TD3 vs SAC vs PPO) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 48 119 Tm (Use this slide if someone asks, "why TD3 and not SAC/PPO?") Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 48 102 Tm (Plain-language version:) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 62 85 Tm (- DDPG learns a deterministic continuous controller from replay-buffer data.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 48 72 Tm (  It is conceptually simple, but in this project it was fragile. It produced) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 48 59 Tm (  high-frequency torque and did not pass fixed local tests reliably.) Tj ET
